TITLE:: RLWalker
summary:: A chord and its voices taking one step at a time: walks without an end, in a Pbind or a beat of reCurrent
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLWalk, Classes/RLVoiceLeading, Classes/RLChordContext, Classes/RLRelationContext, Classes/RCBeat, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
An link::Classes/RLWalk:: is a whole walk, known in advance. A walker holds only where it is: the chord, the voicing, and a count of steps. link::#-step:: moves it by one of its context's transformations or relations, each voice by the least motion (link::Classes/RLVoiceLeading::); where a relation offers several chords it takes the nearest, or what its link::#-chooser:: says.

link::#-asPattern:: gives the frequencies of each chord as a word goes by, the word being a pattern of names: the value of code::\freq:: in a Pbind, or in a beat of reCurrent, which is how a piece plays it. Started again, the pattern goes on from where the walker stands, so the word can be changed while the harmony continues.

Voices that climb or sink for ever (the R, L chain; "Take a Bow") are brought back by octaves once their middle leaves the strong::reach:: around where they started.

code::
~ctx = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12)));
~walker = RLWalker(~ctx, ~ctx.space.major(0), [60, 64, 67], 8.1757989156, 0.75);    // the origin at MIDI note 0
~walker.step(\R).chord;                    // A minor
~walker.voicing;                           // [60, 64, 69]
Pbind(\freq, ~walker.asPattern(Prand([\P, \L, \R], inf)), \dur, 1).play;

// in a piece (reCurrent): the word changes on the next beat, the harmony goes on from where it is
~beat = ~song.layer(\core).addBeat(\harmony, [instrument: \default, dur_flex: 2, freq: ~walker.asPattern(Pseq([\R, \L], inf))]);
~beat.set(\freq, ~walker.asPattern(Pseq([\P, \L], inf)));

// on Cube Dance, where a step may offer six chords; in 19 steps; on the just lattice
~cd = RLRelationContext.cubeDance;
~w = RLWalker(~cd, ~cd.space.major(0), nil, 220, 0.75);
~w.chooser = { |options| options.choose };           // any of the chords offered, not the nearest
Pbind(\freq, ~w.asPattern(Pseq([\U, \U, \P], inf)), \dur, 0.5).play;
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: context
An link::Classes/RLChordContext:: or an link::Classes/RLRelationContext::.
argument:: start
A chord of it.
argument:: voicing
Pitches of the start chord, as many voices as wanted (close position at the origin when nil). Anything that is not the chord is an Error.
argument:: base
The frequency of the origin.
argument:: reach
How far, in octaves, the middle of the voices may go from where it started before they are brought back by an octave; nil for no limit.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: context
METHOD:: chord
METHOD:: voicing
METHOD:: count
Where the walker is, and how many steps it has taken.

METHOD:: base
METHOD:: centre
METHOD:: reach
The frequency of the origin; the middle the voices are kept around, in octaves above the origin; how far they may go from it.

METHOD:: chooser
A Function code::(options, walker) -> chord:: for the steps that offer several chords; nil for the chord the voices reach by the least motion.

METHOD:: step
One step by a name of the context (or a transformation, or a relation). A step that offers no chord is an Error.

METHOD:: next
A step, then the frequencies.

METHOD:: moveTo
Put the walker on a chord: with a voicing of it, or by the least motion from where the voices are.

METHOD:: follow
Gives the walker a shadow on the just lattice that takes every step it takes, from 1/1 (or from a chord given): where the walk really goes, for the lattice page (link::Classes/RLLattice::). For walkers of a chord context of triads (the lattice of 3 and 5) or of the tetrads 4:5:6:7 (the lattice of 3, 5 and 7), stepped by names. Returns the walker.

METHOD:: shadow
METHOD:: latticeChord
The shadow, a walker on the lattice (nil without one); the walker's chord on the lattice, its shadow's or its own.

METHOD:: freqs
METHOD:: middle
The frequencies of the voices; their middle, in octaves above the origin.

METHOD:: asPattern
The frequencies of each chord as a word goes by: first the chord the walker stands on, then one step for each name.
argument:: word
A pattern of names, or an Array taken once. The pattern ends when the word does.
